docker logs

1. docker pull
Downloads a Docker image from Docker Hub (or another registry) to your local machine.
Why?
You need the image before creating and running a container.
Syntax
// docker pull nginx
Example
// docker pull ubuntu
Downloads the latest Ubuntu image.

2. docker run
Creates and starts a new container from an image.
> Why?
Used to run applications inside containers.
> Syntax
docker run [OPTIONS] IMAGE
Example
docker run -d -p 80:80 nginx

Options:
-d → Run in background (detached mode)
-p → Map host port to container port

Runs an Nginx web server.

3. docker push
Uploads a Docker image to Docker Hub or another container registry.

Why?

To share your image or deploy it on another machine.

Syntax
docker push username/myapp:latest
Example
docker push golukumar/myapp:v1

Note: In your list, docker pusz is a typo. The correct command is docker push.

4. docker ps
What?

Lists running containers.

Why?

To see which containers are currently active.

Syntax
docker ps
Example Output
CONTAINER ID   IMAGE    STATUS
abc123         nginx    Up 5 minutes

To see all containers (running + stopped):

docker ps -a
5. docker stop
What?

Stops a running container.

Why?

To safely stop an application.

Syntax
docker stop <container_id>
Example
docker stop abc123
6. docker start
What?

Starts a stopped container.

Why?

Reuse an existing container without creating a new one.

Syntax
docker start <container_id>
Example
docker start abc123
7. docker rm
What?

Removes a stopped container.

Why?

Free disk space and clean up unused containers.

Syntax
docker rm <container_id>
Example
docker rm abc123

To remove a running container forcefully:

docker rm -f abc123
8. docker images
What?

Lists all Docker images stored locally.

Why?

To check which images are available on your system.

Syntax
docker images
Example Output
REPOSITORY   TAG      IMAGE ID
nginx        latest   a1b2c3
ubuntu       latest   d4e5f6
9. docker exec
What?

Runs a command inside an already running container.

Why?

To access the container for debugging or administration.
Syntax
docker exec -it <container_id> bash
Example
docker exec -it abc123 bash
If bash isn't available (e.g., Alpine Linux):
// docker exec -it abc123 sh

10. docker logs
What?
Displays logs generated by a container.
Why?
To troubleshoot errors and monitor application output.
Syntax
docker logs <container_id>
Example
// docker logs abc123
To continuously watch logs:
// docker logs -f abc123